              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kenneth COSSIN"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made this garam masala many times, and I love it! I've modified this recipe to replace the peppers with 1/4 teaspoon of cayenne pepper, and added in 1/4 teaspoon of nutmeg and 1/4 teaspoon of ginger. I love the subtle tastes that the nutmeg and ginger add. I typically don't have fennel, so I leave that out.",
            "datePublished": "2021-09-08T20:05:56.280Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jordan VanDijk"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A good recipe, nice and flavourful, but needs some work. I think it was quite heavy on the coriander seed. It overpowered all other flavours in my final dish. The turmeric was a little heavy too. I'll redo this recipe with those two ingredients dialed back a bit. Thanks!",
            "datePublished": "2015-04-30T20:58:00.320Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Scott Sealy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used already ground spices as finding whole seeds is challenging. It’s a very nice recipe. It makes a good amount too. I added a big dash of ground nutmeg and Aleppo pepper instead of whole chilis as well. I’ll keep this on hand for sure.",
            "datePublished": "2024-08-16T00:44:33.151Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "David Starnes"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Delicious and fragrant! The hardest part was finding my fennel seed after making mukhwas, candied fennel seeds:). Oh yeah, I put them in a blue Mason jar, even labeling it. The spice mix bloomed perfectly! And, ground up in the spice grinder, perfectly fine. Put it through a fine sieve. I only wish I could use fresher tumeric, the dried spice blend loses its yummy tumeric flavor and anterior and retrograde beautiful aroma once ground. Who doesn’t care about yellow orange fingertips? Me. I’ll probably dehydrate much fresher tumeric.",
            "datePublished": "2024-09-16T16:37:19.922Z"
          },
          {
